<section id="faq-2" class="faq-2 section light-background">
    <!-- Section Title -->
    <div class="container section-title">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    </div><!-- End Section Title -->

    <div class="container">
        <div class="row justify-content-center">
            <div class="col-lg-10">
                <div class="accordion" id="faqAccordion">
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h3 class="accordion-header" id="faqHeading01">
                            <button class="accordion-button" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#faqCollapse01" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faqCollapse01">
                                What does a Dynamics 365 CRM implementation include?
                            </button>
                        </h3>
                        <div id="faqCollapse01" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" aria-labelledby="faqHeading01" data-bs-parent="#faqAccordion">
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                A Dynamics 365 CRM implementation typically includes requirements discovery, process design, environment configuration, security planning, data preparation, integrations, testing, user training, and go-live support. The exact scope depends on the Dynamics 365 apps involved, the organization’s processes, existing systems, data quality, and adoption needs. A structured implementation plan helps establish priorities, responsibilities, validation steps, and a support approach before deployment.
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h3 class="accordion-header" id="faqHeading02">
                            <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#faqCollapse02" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faqCollapse02">
                                How can Dynamics 365 CRM be customized for our business?
                            </button>
                        </h3>
                        <div id="faqCollapse02" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="faqHeading02" data-bs-parent="#faqAccordion">
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                Dynamics 365 CRM can be tailored using standard configuration such as tables, columns, forms, views, dashboards, business rules, security roles, and business process flows. Where configuration is not sufficient, organizations may use Power Apps, Power Automate, JavaScript, plugins, custom connectors, or other supported extensions. Customization should begin with clear requirements and favor maintainable solutions over unnecessary complexity.
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h3 class="accordion-header" id="faqHeading03">
                            <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#faqCollapse03" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faqCollapse03">
                                Can Dynamics 365 CRM integrate with our existing business systems?
                            </button>
                        </h3>
                        <div id="faqCollapse03" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="faqHeading03" data-bs-parent="#faqAccordion">
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                Yes. Dynamics 365 CRM can connect with ERP, finance, marketing, customer service, identity, document, and other business systems through native connectors, Dataverse APIs, webhooks, custom connectors, middleware, or integration services. A reliable integration design defines system ownership, data mappings, authentication, synchronization frequency, error handling, monitoring, and reconciliation so connected processes remain understandable and supportable.
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h3 class="accordion-header" id="faqHeading04">
                            <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#faqCollapse04" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faqCollapse04">
                                How does Dynamics 365 CRM data migration work?
                            </button>
                        </h3>
                        <div id="faqCollapse04" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="faqHeading04" data-bs-parent="#faqAccordion">
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                A Dynamics 365 CRM migration normally begins with source-system profiling and data ownership review. The process then covers cleansing, deduplication, field mapping, transformation, validation, test loads, reconciliation, and cutover planning. Migration teams should also account for relationships, historical records, attachments, security, integration dependencies, and user acceptance. The final approach depends on source systems, volume, data quality, and the required business continuity window.
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h3 class="accordion-header" id="faqHeading05">
                            <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#faqCollapse05" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faqCollapse05">
                                How should security be managed in Dynamics 365 CRM?
                            </button>
                        </h3>
                        <div id="faqCollapse05" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="faqHeading05" data-bs-parent="#faqAccordion">
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                Dynamics 365 CRM security should reflect business roles, data sensitivity, geographic requirements, and administrative responsibilities. Planning commonly includes Microsoft Entra ID authentication, security roles, business units, teams, record ownership, field security, auditing, environment controls, and least-privilege access. Integrations and custom extensions also require secure authentication, appropriate permissions, protected secrets, monitoring, and periodic access reviews.
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h3 class="accordion-header" id="faqHeading06">
                            <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#faqCollapse06" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faqCollapse06">
                                Can Dynamics 365 CRM support custom reporting and analytics?
                            </button>
                        </h3>
                        <div id="faqCollapse06" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="faqHeading06" data-bs-parent="#faqAccordion">
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                Dynamics 365 CRM supports views, charts, dashboards, and reports based on Dataverse data. Power BI can extend this capability with interactive analysis, cross-system reporting, and role-specific insights. Effective reporting starts by defining the decisions and measures that matter, confirming data ownership and quality, and controlling access to sensitive information. Reports should be tested with users to ensure that definitions and filters are understood.
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h3 class="accordion-header" id="faqHeading07">
                            <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#faqCollapse07" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faqCollapse07">
                                How can Power Automate be used with Dynamics 365 CRM?
                            </button>
                        </h3>
                        <div id="faqCollapse07" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="faqHeading07" data-bs-parent="#faqAccordion">
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                Power Automate can automate notifications, approvals, task creation, record updates, document processes, and connections to other services. Flows should be designed around clear triggers, business ownership, exception handling, retry behavior, service limits, and appropriate permissions. For complex or high-volume processing, the solution should be evaluated against plugins, scheduled processes, or other integration patterns to ensure suitable reliability and maintainability.
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h3 class="accordion-header" id="faqHeading08">
                            <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#faqCollapse08" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faqCollapse08">
                                What training is needed for Dynamics 365 CRM users?
                            </button>
                        </h3>
                        <div id="faqCollapse08" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="faqHeading08" data-bs-parent="#faqAccordion">
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                Training should reflect each role’s processes rather than only demonstrating generic product features. Typical topics include navigation, records, views, activities, business processes, dashboards, data quality, security responsibilities, and organization-specific customizations. Effective adoption also includes guided practice, role-based materials, support during rollout, feedback collection, and refresher guidance when processes or Dynamics 365 capabilities change.
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h3 class="accordion-header" id="faqHeading09">
                            <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#faqCollapse09" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faqCollapse09">
                                What ongoing support does Dynamics 365 CRM require?
                            </button>
                        </h3>
                        <div id="faqCollapse09" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="faqHeading09" data-bs-parent="#faqAccordion">
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                Ongoing support may include issue investigation, configuration changes, user assistance, integration monitoring, data-quality reviews, security administration, release testing, and performance analysis. A support model should define response priorities, escalation routes, ownership, documentation, and change control. Regular reviews can identify recurring issues and improvements while helping keep customizations, automations, and integrations aligned with current business needs.
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h3 class="accordion-header" id="faqHeading10">
                            <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#faqCollapse10" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faqCollapse10">
                                How should Dynamics 365 CRM upgrades be planned?
                            </button>
                        </h3>
                        <div id="faqCollapse10" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="faqHeading10" data-bs-parent="#faqAccordion">
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                Upgrade planning should review release notes, customizations, integrations, security, reports, automated flows, and dependent processes. Changes should be tested in a representative non-production environment, with regression scenarios and user validation defined in advance. Dynamics 365 online follows Microsoft’s release model, while on-premises environments have separate product-version and infrastructure considerations. Documentation and rollback or remediation plans help reduce operational risk.
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h3 class="accordion-header" id="faqHeading11">
                            <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#faqCollapse11" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faqCollapse11">
                                Should we use Dynamics 365 CRM in the cloud or on-premises?
                            </button>
                        </h3>
                        <div id="faqCollapse11" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="faqHeading11" data-bs-parent="#faqAccordion">
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                The choice depends on regulatory obligations, infrastructure strategy, integration requirements, operational skills, data residency, update preferences, and the capabilities required by the business. Dynamics 365 online provides Microsoft-managed cloud services and a continuous release model. On-premises deployment can provide different infrastructure and control options but requires organizations to manage servers, updates, availability, security, and maintenance. An assessment should compare requirements rather than assume one model fits every organization.
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h3 class="accordion-header" id="faqHeading12">
                            <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#faqCollapse12" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faqCollapse12">
                                How is Dynamics 365 CRM project pricing determined?
                            </button>
                        </h3>
                        <div id="faqCollapse12" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="faqHeading12" data-bs-parent="#faqAccordion">
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                Pricing depends on the required Dynamics 365 apps, number of users, environments, configuration and development scope, integrations, data migration, reporting, testing, training, and ongoing support. A useful estimate follows discovery of requirements, current systems, data condition, dependencies, and delivery priorities. The proposal should distinguish implementation work, third-party or Microsoft licensing, optional services, and recurring support so costs are clear.
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
<!--end faq2-->
